 \abstract{
 Short-term precipitation forecasting over Dublin is challenging because of the strong temporal variability of rainfall and the maritime influence on regional weather conditions. Using hourly European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts Reanalysis v5 (ERA5) data from 2004 to 2021 subsampled every 6~h without temporal aggregation, this study systematically evaluates reference forecasts, XGBoost, Transformer, deterministic bidirectional long short-term memory (BiLSTM) models, and probabilistic recurrent models for 6-hour-ahead prediction of hourly ERA5-derived regional-mean precipitation over Dublin, expressed as an hourly-mean rate in mm\,h$^{-1}$ rather than as a total over the next 6~h. Model performance is assessed using repeated training with five fixed random seeds, rainfall-regime and threshold-based evaluation, probabilistic metrics, SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) attribution, and feature ablation. BiLSTM-Attention achieved the lowest mean test root mean squared error (RMSE) of 0.2429~mm\,h$^{-1}$, closely followed by XGBoost at 0.2439~mm\,h$^{-1}$, while bootstrap analysis showed no clear difference between the two models for the full test set. \textcolor{red}{Under upper-tail precipitation, however, XGBoost achieved lower error, while predicted amplitudes were compressed. Validation-selected event cutoffs recovered some detections but incurred high false-alarm ratios, and tested loss and weighting strategies exposed model-dependent tradeoffs rather than resolving upper-tail underestimation.} \textcolor{red}{Under fixed high-intensity decision thresholds, learning-model CSI was lower than Persistence, limiting their interpretation as event-warning systems.} The probabilistic recurrent models produced empirical 95\% coverage close to the nominal level over the full test set, but coverage deteriorated markedly for upper-tail precipitation events, revealing substantial undercoverage. Matched five-seed SHAP and ablation analyses in two architectures revealed regime-dependent model reliance and overlapping predictive information among precipitation-related inputs, rather than a universally dominant predictor. These results highlight the importance of regime-specific evaluation, repeated-run robustness analysis, and complementary interpretability methods in regional precipitation forecasting.
- \textcolor{red}{BiLSTM-Attention achieved the numerically lowest RMSE on the original test period, but its advantage over XGBoost was small and did not persist consistently across the rolling-origin evaluation. All six models in the rolling-origin evaluation retained positive climatological skill across four test periods, while upper-tail undercoverage persisted.}}
+ \textcolor{red}{BiLSTM-Attention achieved the numerically lowest RMSE on the original test period, but its advantage over XGBoost was small and did not persist consistently across the rolling-origin evaluation. All six models in the rolling-origin evaluation retained positive climatological skill across four test periods, while upper-tail undercoverage persisted.} \textcolor{red}{An alternative precipitation-appropriate likelihood improved overall probabilistic skill and eliminated negative prediction bounds by construction, but substantial upper-tail undercoverage remained.}}
 
 \keywords{Short-term precipitation forecasting, ERA5 reanalysis data, Model robustness, Probabilistic forecasting, Predictor attribution}
 
