+The likelihood comparison reused the Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention backbone: one bidirectional LSTM layer with 64 units, dropout 0.2 and additive temporal attention. Inputs, chronological partitions, five seeds (42, 123, 7, 2024, 99), Adam settings, batch size 32, 30-epoch cap and validation-loss early stopping (patience 10) were matched. Gaussian training used standardized targets, whereas Tobit and Bernoulli--Gamma training used precipitation in mm. Output heads, likelihood losses and target scales therefore changed together; this was not a strict single-factor experiment in which only the output distribution changed. These established probability families are alternatives within one tested network, not new forecasting methods.
+
+For the Tobit alternative, $Y=\max(0,Z)$ with $Z\sim\mathcal N(\mu,s^2)$ \citep{tobin1958limited}. For Bernoulli--Gamma, $P(Y=0)=1-p$ and $f(y)=p\,y^{a-1}e^{-y/b}/[\Gamma(a)b^a]$ for $y>0$ \citep{cannon2008bernoulli}. The Tobit outputs are $\mu$ and $s=\operatorname{softplus}(h_s)+10^{-3}$; BG outputs are $p=\operatorname{clip}(\operatorname{sigmoid}(h_p),10^{-5},1-10^{-5})$, $a=\operatorname{softplus}(h_a)+10^{-2}$ and $b=\operatorname{softplus}(h_b)+10^{-3}$. Each model minimizes its sample negative log likelihood: Tobit uses $-\log\Phi(-\mu/s)$ at zero and the negative Gaussian log density at positive targets; BG uses $-\log(1-p)$ at zero and $-\log p-\log f_\Gamma(y;a,b)$ at positive targets. These point-mass terms are not a Gaussian density evaluated at zero. For numerical stability, the Tobit zero probability is clipped to $[10^{-12},1]$ before taking its logarithm; conditional Gamma quantile probabilities are clipped to $[0,1-10^{-9}]$ in the saved implementation.
+
+Here $\phi$ and $\Phi$ denote the standard-normal density and CDF, and $G_{a,b}$ and $f_\Gamma$ denote the Gamma CDF and density with shape $a$ and scale $b$. For $0<q<1$, Tobit quantiles are $\max(0,\mu+s\Phi^{-1}(q))$. BG quantiles equal zero for $q\leq1-p$ and $G_{a,b}^{-1}((q-1+p)/p)$ otherwise. For a positive threshold $u$, exceedance probabilities are $1-\Phi((u-\mu)/s)$ for Tobit and $p[1-G_{a,b}(u)]$ for BG; their predictive means are $\mu\Phi(\mu/s)+s\phi(\mu/s)$ and $pab$, respectively. The Gaussian rerun transforms its standardized mean and standard deviation back to precipitation units before scoring.
+
+The main alternative-likelihood setting used dry $=0$, meaning exact-zero precipitation after the archived $10^{-6}$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ numerical-zero tolerance; every positive ERA5 target above that numerical tolerance entered the positive component. A seed-42 exploration of dry $=0$, 0.001 and 0.01 preceded expansion to the other seeds. No verifiable prespecification record was identified, and dry $=0$ did not give the best validation or test CRPS for either alternative distribution. Dry $=0.001$ has five seeds, whereas dry $=0.01$ has only seed 42. This exploration is disclosed rather than represented as an independent, prespecified choice.
