 \ReviewerComment{6.The Gaussian assumption produces negative prediction bounds and severe undercoverage for heavy rainfall. Please evaluate a precipitation-appropriate distribution or provide stronger justification for the chosen formulation.}
 
-\PendingResponse
-
-\ChangeLocation
+\AuthorResponse{We agree that the unbounded Gaussian baseline can yield physically inappropriate negative bounds and that nominal overall coverage does not ensure upper-tail reliability. We added Gaussian, zero-censored Gaussian (Tobit) and Bernoulli--Gamma reruns in the same Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention backbone, with matched inputs, partitions, seeds and training budget. The output heads, likelihood losses and target scales differ: Gaussian uses standardized targets, while the alternatives use precipitation units. We therefore do not describe this as a strict distribution-only single-factor comparison.
+
+Bernoulli--Gamma improves full CRPS from 0.0961 to 0.0773~mm\,h$^{-1}$ (about 19.6\%), with matched full-score seed-$t$ and temporal block intervals supporting lower scores. Tobit gives 0.0847. Bernoulli--Gamma also reduces IS95 by about 29\% and Width95 by 32\%, while overall Cov95 is similar (0.9565 versus 0.9560). Both alternatives eliminate negative bounds through non-negative support. However, upper-tail Cov95 improves only from 0.4941 to 0.6445 (Tobit 0.5454), well below 0.95. Q95 and 1.0 twCRPS improve by only about 2.8\% and 1.9\%. Although BG has lower overall CRPS than Tobit, their high-threshold block intervals include zero. We retain the adverse observed-subset CRPS diagnostics, which favour Gaussian, but do not use those outcome-conditioned scores to rank complete forecast distributions.
+
+The exact-zero setting includes the archived numerical tolerance. A seed-42 exploration preceded expansion to five seeds, but no verifiable prespecification record exists; dry 0 did not minimize validation or test CRPS. Dry 0.001 has five seeds and dry 0.01 only seed 42. Tobit is more sensitive to this choice. Missing same-definition Tobit sensitivity full intervals are explicitly unavailable, rather than replaced with historical approximate-score intervals.
+
+Formal CRPS and its paired intervals use the archived full numerical-integral scores. Independent closed-form validation agrees to four decimals, with maximum per-seed difference $4.31615\times10^{-5}$~mm\,h$^{-1}$; we do not call the original integration closed-form or exact. The paired non-circular block procedure uses block 28, 5,000 replicates and seed 42, after averaging five per-case scores. It retains 3,948 positions rather than truncating to 3,939. All intervals are pointwise and unadjusted; seed-$t$ and test-sample uncertainty remain separate. Raw NLL values are not used for comparisons between continuous and mixed likelihoods on different training scales.
+
+Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:probabilistic_output} defines the alternatives, Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:experimental_setup} defines the scores and resampling, and Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:r2m6_likelihood_results} reports the results. Table~\ref{manuscript-tab:probabilistic_results}, Part A1 preserves the original Gaussian-model scores, Part A2 separately adds existing q16 interval reruns, and Part B gives q11 likelihood reruns. Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m6_q16_intervals}--\ref{supp-stab:r2m6_conditional_history} supply per-seed, pairwise, weighted-score and sensitivity evidence. Discussion, Abstract, contributions and Conclusion now state both the improvement and the unresolved upper-tail deficit; no benefit is extrapolated to other networks or the rolling-origin periods.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Sections~\ref{manuscript-sec:probabilistic_output},~\ref{manuscript-sec:experimental_setup} and~\ref{manuscript-sec:r2m6_likelihood_results}; Table~\ref{manuscript-tab:probabilistic_results} Parts A1/A2/B; Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m6_q16_intervals}--\ref{supp-stab:r2m6_conditional_history}; Abstract, contributions, Discussion and Conclusion. Final page and line numbers pending.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Matched backbone and changed training scales}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The likelihood comparison reused the Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention backbone: one bidirectional LSTM layer with 64 units, dropout 0.2 and additive temporal attention. Inputs, chronological partitions, five seeds (42, 123, 7, 2024, 99), Adam settings, batch size 32, 30-epoch cap and validation-loss early stopping (patience 10) were matched. Gaussian training used standardized targets, whereas Tobit and Bernoulli--Gamma training used precipitation in mm. Output heads, likelihood losses and target scales therefore changed together; this was not a strict single-factor experiment in which only the output distribution changed. These established probability families are alternatives within one tested network, not new forecasting methods.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Exploratory dry definition}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The main alternative-likelihood setting used dry $=0$, meaning exact-zero precipitation after the archived $10^{-6}$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ numerical-zero tolerance; every positive ERA5 target above that numerical tolerance entered the positive component. A seed-42 exploration of dry $=0$, 0.001 and 0.01 preceded expansion to the other seeds. No verifiable prespecification record was identified, and dry $=0$ did not give the best validation or test CRPS for either alternative distribution. Dry $=0.001$ has five seeds, whereas dry $=0.01$ has only seed 42. This exploration is disclosed rather than represented as an independent, prespecified choice.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Formal full-score definition}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{For the likelihood reruns, full CRPS and threshold-weighted CRPS were evaluated from the predictive CDF on all 3,939 test targets. The formal full scores use the archived numerical-integration column, not the historical 199-quantile approximation; Gaussian integration was over $[-4,8]$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ and the non-negative families over $[0,8]$~mm\,h$^{-1}$. Independent closed-form calculations differed from the full scores by at most $4.31615\times10^{-5}$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ per seed and gave the same four-decimal model means. The integration scores are not described as closed-form or mathematically exact. Conditioning a score on observed wet or upper-tail cases is retained only as a diagnostic, not as a proper criterion for ranking the full predictive distribution.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Intervals, Brier event and NLL boundary}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{For Brier scores, the archived wet-event code used $y>0.1$~mm\,h$^{-1}$. No test target equalled 0.1, so this event set equals $y\geq0.1$ on these 3,939 targets (854 events); this equivalence is not assumed for other datasets. Q95 remained training-derived. Coverage and mean widths were reported for central 90\% and 95\% intervals using saved quantiles; Width90 is the mean of $q_{0.95}-q_{0.05}$ and is not Width95. Negative-bound frequency is the proportion of lower 95\% bounds below zero. Raw NLL values were not used to rank continuous Gaussian densities against mixed discrete--continuous likelihoods, particularly across the different training scales.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Actual 3,948-position paired block procedure}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The likelihood comparison used paired non-circular moving blocks of 28 six-hourly samples, 5,000 replicates and resampling seed 42. The five per-case scores were averaged before applying the same resampled indices to each compared model; predictions were not averaged before scoring. Each replicate retained 141 complete blocks, giving 3,948 positions, without truncating to the original 3,939 targets. This implementation differs from a length-$n$ bootstrap and changes tail-block contributions; non-circular sampling also weights endpoints differently. Pointwise 95\% percentile intervals were not adjusted for multiple comparisons. Paired seed-$t$ intervals (five seeds, four degrees of freedom) describe training variability separately from test-sample block intervals; neither interval combines both sources of uncertainty. Only intervals matched to the archived full scores are used for full CRPS.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Likelihood rerun results}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{In the matched likelihood reruns, full CRPS was 0.0961 for Gaussian, 0.0847 for Tobit and 0.0773~mm\,h$^{-1}$ for Bernoulli--Gamma. Relative to Gaussian, Bernoulli--Gamma reduced full CRPS by approximately 19.6\%, IS95 by 29\% and Width95 by 32\%; overall Cov95 remained similar (0.9565 versus 0.9560). Both non-negative likelihoods eliminated negative bounds by construction. Bernoulli--Gamma increased upper-tail Cov95 from 0.4941 to 0.6445, still far below 0.95; Tobit gave 0.5454. The observed upper-tail reliability deficit was therefore reduced but not resolved.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Residual upper-tail and adverse conditional diagnostics}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Bernoulli--Gamma reduced the Q95 and 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$ threshold-weighted CRPS by only about 2.8\% and 1.9\%, respectively, on all test cases. Its full CRPS was lower than Tobit under both paired uncertainty summaries, but their high-threshold block intervals included zero despite seed-$t$ intervals excluding zero. The historical observed-wet and upper-tail conditional CRPS diagnostics instead ranked Bernoulli--Gamma worse than Gaussian; these outcome-conditioned diagnostics are not proper scores for the full predictive distribution. We retain this adverse result separately from the full/twCRPS comparison.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Sensitivity and missing matched intervals}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Tobit was more sensitive to the explored dry definitions than Bernoulli--Gamma. The dry $=0.001$ five-seed full scores are retained alongside the main setting; a matching Tobit dry-0.001 minus dry-0 full block interval was not saved and is not inferred from approximate-CRPS intervals. The dry $=0.01$ result is only a historical seed-42 exploration, not a five-seed robustness result. Historical validation CRPS did not favour dry $=0$ for either family.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Interpretation of the likelihood comparison}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{An alternative precipitation-appropriate likelihood improved overall probabilistic skill and eliminated negative prediction bounds by construction, but substantial upper-tail undercoverage remained. The comparison does not isolate the likelihood family from changes in output parameterization, loss and training-target scale. It supports the tested Bernoulli--Gamma formulation within this backbone, not a general claim that it improves every architecture or rare-event diagnostic.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Scope and uncertainty limitations}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The alternative-likelihood evidence is limited to one Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention backbone and the original chronological test period. Target scales differed between Gaussian and the non-negative likelihoods, the dry definition lacks a verifiable prespecification record, and the dry-0.01 check has only one seed. No rolling-origin Bernoulli--Gamma test was performed; these results cannot be transferred to the 12-year Gaussian-model evaluation. The retained 3,948-position block implementation and pointwise intervals limit uncertainty interpretation. Better overall scores and non-negative support did not eliminate systematic upper-tail undercoverage.}
+}
 
